# {{ project_name }}

Generated by TAAS from the approved test corpus. **The corpus in SQLite is the system
of record** — editing a file here does not change it.

## What is generated, and what is safe to edit

| Path | Regenerated? |
|---|---|
| `tests/*.spec.ts` | Yes, from the corpus |
| `pages/*.page.ts` | Yes, from the UI model |
| `fixtures/auth.ts` | Yes |
| `playwright.config.ts`, `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `.env.example` | Written once |

**Every file here is safe to edit.** A regeneration compares each file against the
hash recorded when it was last written: if you have changed it, the file is skipped
and reported as stale rather than overwritten. Nothing you write is lost — but the
file will stop tracking the corpus until you resolve it.

## Running

```bash
npm install
npx playwright install --with-deps
cp .env.example .env      # then fill it in
npx playwright test
```

Pinned to `@playwright/test` **{{ playwright_version }}**. Install the browsers for
that exact version on the CI agent: a version drift between this project and the
agent's browsers is the classic cause of "passes locally, fails in CI".

## Selecting suites

Tests carry the tags of the case they came from. Jenkins can select by tag expression:

```bash
npx playwright test --grep "@smoke"
npx playwright test --grep-invert "@slow"
```

## Reading a failure

Every test names its case identifier and Jira key, in the title and in an annotation
that reaches the JUnit XML. A red test in CI carries its own provenance — you should
never need to query the corpus to learn which story a failure belongs to.

## Unverified locators

Locators marked `UNVERIFIED` were derived from the design model and have not been
confirmed against a running application. They are the first thing to check when a test
fails for a reason that looks structural.

